9,000 of Drones From a Toronto Startup That Won't Name Its Founders · Ottawasted (https://ottawasted.ca/story/contract-009-ottawa-quietly-bought-489000-of-drones-from-a-toronto-start-493048)\nciteturn23search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 days ago; Crawled: 4 days ago; Content type: text/plain; \nDefence as the end user, and a Toronto supplier called FPDynamics Advanced Robotics. The commodity code is blunt — \"military drone.\" The title is the most interesting part: Low-Cost Uncrewed Aircraft Systems (UAS) Contract 009. Nine contracts, one solicitation number, W8703-270094. This is the ninth. Spread across every person in Canada, $489,000 is a little more than a penny each. Set against the roughly $50 million Ottawa said in September it was putting into its first wave of Canadian drone contracts, it is under one per cent. Set against FPDynamics itself, it is the largest cheque the company has ever received from the federal government — by a factor of about fourteen. Here is the trail. In December 2025, DND's Innovation for Defence Excellence and Security program gave FPDynamics Advanced Robotics Ltd. $35,000 under a contest called \"Drone surge: Scaling tactical Uncrewed Aerial Systems manufacturing for modern defence.\" The project title filed with the grant is FP-X: Canada's Low-Cost, Modular, and AI-Enabled UAS. Round one money. Thirty companies made the cut. On May 8, 2026, the same program paid the company $339,480. That is round two. In July 2026, National Defence launched the Defence Drone Initiative — a rolling marketplace meant to pre-qualify Canadian suppliers of uncrewed and autonomous systems and shorten the distance between a prototype and a purchase order. Defence Minister David J. McGuinty framed it as a speed problem: \"The future of defence will belong to those who can innovate and adapt the fastest.\" The supply arrangement, W8703-270055, runs 59 months, to July 31, 2031. Hundreds of companies applied. By August, FPDynamics was on the qualified list. On September 10, the Prime Minister's Office published a backgrounder alongside drone-production commitments made with Ukraine. It named six suppliers receiving initial awards worth up to $50 million: AVSS, Volatus Aerospace, Draganfly, Objexis AI, Twenty20 Insight and Beonyx. Draganfly's CEO Cameron Chell ... million. At that same ceiling, $489,000 buys fewer than a hundred airframes before you count ground stations, spares, training and documentation — all of which this contract explicitly includes. So the contract is small. The runway is not. It runs to October 2, 2031, inside a marketplace designed from the start to turn small call-ups into large ones, modelled — the government says — on Ukraine's Brave1 platform. Which leaves the question the paperwork does not answer. Ottawa has decided speed matters more than ceremony, and is now writing... National Defence Open Government — Grant record: FPDynamics Advanced Robotics Ltd., $35,000, IDEaS (Dec 15, 2025) Open Government — Grants and Contributions search (FPDynamics Advanced Robotics Ltd.) Prime Minister of Canada — Backgrounder: Canada and Ukraine to scale up drone production, build up Canada's defence industries Draganfly Selected by Government of Canada to Supply Low-Cost Tactical ISR UAS to the Canadian Armed Forces Volatus Aerospace Awarded Canadian Defence Contract for Tactical ISR Uncrewed Aircraft Systems for up to 5,000 systems True North Strategic Review — This Week in Defence (10/05/26): More Low-Cost UAS Contracts FPDynamics — company site FPDynamics — FP1 Autonomous Drone System CanadaBuys — W8703-270055 Defence Drone Initiative (DDI) Marketplace tender notice Follow the money The raw government record → Everything FPDynamics Advanced Robotics has been paid → What else Department of Public Works and Government Services (PSPC) spends on → All investigations → Swipe → FPDynamics Advanced...--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nhttps://webfiles.thecse